      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${4}^{8}≡$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{4}^{8}={4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 7+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot {4}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{8} & ≡{4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{4}^{8}≡2\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$",
