    "q-213073": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213073",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n${12}^{4}\\text{ mod }8=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{12}^{4}=(3\\cdot 4)^{4}={3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 8+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ {4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 8+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{4} & ≡{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{4}\\text{ mod }8=0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
