    "q-213075": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213075",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n${36}^{4}\\text{ mod }11=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{36}^{4}=(9\\cdot 4)^{4}={9}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2} & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 11+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 11+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{36}^{4} & ≡{9}^{2}\\cdot {9}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡400\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 11+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{36}^{4}\\text{ mod }11=4.\n$$",
