    "q-213058": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213058",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${21}^{4}≡$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{21}^{4}=(3\\cdot 7)^{4}={3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 6+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ {7}^{2} & ≡49\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 6+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{21}^{4} & ≡{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 6+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{21}^{4}≡3\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-213058-a",
