    "q-213172": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213172",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${14}^{14}?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{14}^{14}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & ≡10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{14}^{2} & ≡{4}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {14}^{6} & ≡{({14}^{2})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{6}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡216\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡21\\cdot 10+6 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡21\\cdot 0+6 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${14}^{14}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{14}^{14}={({14}^{6})}^{2}\\cdot {14}^{2}\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{14}^{14} & ≡{({14}^{6})}^{2}\\cdot {14}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(6)^{2}\\cdot 6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡216\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡21\\cdot 10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡21\\cdot 0+6 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{14}^{14}\\text{ mod }10=6\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${14}^{14}$ is $6.$",
