    "q-213186": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213186",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${29}^{13}?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{29}^{13}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & ≡20+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{29}^{2} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${29}^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{29}^{13}={({29}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot 29\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{29}^{13} & ≡{({29}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot 29\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{6}\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{29}^{13}\\text{ mod }10=9\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${29}^{13}$ is $9.$",
