    "q-213082": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213082",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${19}^{19}?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{19}^{19}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & ≡10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{19}^{2} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${19}^{19}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{19}^{19}={({19}^{2})}^{9}\\cdot 19\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{19}^{19} & ≡{({19}^{2})}^{9}\\cdot 19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{9}\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{19}^{19}\\text{ mod }10=9\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${19}^{19}$ is $9.$",
